course: biology
lesson: 320
title: Bears up close: noses, claws, dens, the honey myth and bear safety
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A bear lives by its nose. It lifts its head into the wind and reads the air the way you read a book, smelling food, cubs and danger far away.
explain: It walks flat on the whole sole of its foot like we do, with five long claws on each paw that never pull in, and it spends winter in a den, sleeping off the hungry months.
explain: Cartoons give bears a honey pot, but real bears raid bees' nests as much for the fat juicy grubs as for the honey, and most bears eat far more berries and roots than meat.
explain: Bears are wonderful to see from far away and dangerous up close. Where bears live, people follow the rangers' rules: keep food locked away, make noise on trails, stay together and never run.
fact: A bear's sense of smell is one of the best of any animal, said to be several times better than a bloodhound's; the inside of its nose has a huge folded area full of smell sensors, and a polar bear is reported to smell a seal from more than a kilometre away.
fact: Bears have five toes with a claw on each paw, twenty claws in all, and the claws do not pull in like a cat's.
fact: Grizzly claws can be about 10 centimetres long for digging, while black bears have shorter, curved claws for climbing.
fact: Bears walk on the whole sole of the foot, like us, which gives them balance and lets them stand on two legs; but a bear's biggest toe is on the outside of its foot, the opposite of ours.
fact: Bears dig winter dens under tree roots, in hillsides or under snow; inside, black bears can go months without eating, drinking, peeing or pooing, and their bodies recycle waste into useful protein, scientists reported.
fact: A bear's winter sleep is lighter than a dormouse's: its body cools only a few degrees and it can wake fast, while a dormouse cools almost to the temperature of its nest; whether to call the bear's sleep true hibernation is still argued.
fact: Bear cubs are born in the den in midwinter, tiny and blind, often under half a kilogram, and grow fast on rich milk before spring; a newborn giant panda is about a thousand times lighter than its mother.
fact: Real bears raid bees' nests for the grubs as much as for the honey, because the grubs are full of protein and fat; thick fur protects most of the bear from stings, but not its nose.
fact: Most bears eat mostly plants, such as berries, roots, nuts and grass; a grizzly in late summer is reported to eat many thousands of berries a day, and only the polar bear eats mostly meat.
fact: Where bears live, the rules are to keep food and rubbish in bear-proof boxes, never feed a bear, make noise on trails and stay with the group.
fact: A bear that learns people mean food can become dangerous, and rangers say «a fed bear is a dead bear», because problem bears are often moved or killed.
fact: If you see a bear: stay calm, keep far away, never run, stay with the grown-ups and follow the rangers' advice; running can make a bear chase, and bears can run about 50 kilometres an hour, faster than any person.
fact: Most bears avoid people and leave if they hear them coming.
fact: Climbing a tree is not a safe escape, because black bears climb very well and young brown bears can too; advice for meeting a bear differs for each kind of bear, so park rangers explain it for their area.
exercise: Why does a bear lift its nose into the wind? | To smell food, cubs and danger far away | To listen better through its big wet nose | To cool its nose down on a hot summer day | To scare the birds away from its berry bushes
exercise: Why is a bear's sense of smell so good? | Its nose has a huge folded area full of smell sensors | It smells through the pads on its paws as it walks along | It has three nostrils | It licks the air like a snake
exercise: How many claws does a bear have on all four paws? | 20 | 16 | 10 | 18
exercise: How are a bear's claws different from a cat's? | They do not pull in | They pull in all the way | They are made of soft skin | They fall off every winter
exercise: Why are grizzly claws long while black bear claws are short and curved? | Grizzlies dig a lot, black bears climb a lot | Black bears bite and chew their claws when they are bored | Grizzlies are older, so their claws grew longer | Grizzly claws grow only in the cold of winter
exercise: Why does a bear's footprint look like a person's? | It walks on the whole sole of its foot, like us | It has only two big toes on each foot, just like us | It wears shoes in winter | It walks on tiptoe like a dog
exercise: Where is a bear's biggest toe? | On the outside of its foot | On the inside, like ours | In the middle | On the back of the heel
exercise: Where do bears make their winter dens? | Under tree roots, in hillsides or under snow | High up in the tree tops, in big nests of sticks | Under water in lakes | In birds' nests
exercise: What can black bears go without for months in the den? | Eating, drinking, peeing and pooing | Breathing | Their heartbeat | Their fur
exercise: How is a bear's winter sleep different from a dormouse's? | The bear cools only a few degrees and can wake fast | The dormouse sleeps lightly and wakes up every hour to eat | The bear cools to ice and cannot wake | The bear never sleeps at all in winter
exercise: How big is a newborn bear cub? | Tiny and blind, often under half a kilogram | About the size of a dog, and it can walk at once | As big as its mother | About 10 kilograms
exercise: When are bear cubs born? | In midwinter, in the den | In midsummer, in the river | In spring, up a tall tree | In autumn, at the salmon run
exercise: What do bears most want from a bees' nest besides honey? | The fat, juicy bee grubs | The wax to build dens | The bees as pets | The wood of the hive
exercise: What do most bears mostly eat? | Plants, such as berries, roots, nuts and grass | Mostly honey, with a few bees in it now and then | Fish every day | Only other bears
exercise: Which bear eats mostly meat? | The polar bear | The big brown bear | The little sun bear | The giant panda
exercise: Why should campers keep food in bear-proof boxes? | So bears do not learn that people mean food | Because bears are allergic to tins and could get ill | To keep the food warmer and fresher overnight | So that the rangers can count it every morning
exercise: What should you do if you see a bear? | Stay calm, keep far away, never run and stay with the grown-ups | Run as fast as you can and climb the nearest tall tree to be safe | Go closer for a photo | Throw it your sandwich
exercise: Why is running from a bear a bad idea? | It can make the bear chase, and it is faster than any person | Running makes you sweat and smell of honey, which bears love | Bears hate the sound of trainers | Bears only chase people who walk
test: In Winnie the Pooh, Pooh eats nothing but honey from a pot. What do real bears do at a bees' nest? | Eat the grubs as much as the honey, and they eat lots of other foods too | Eat only honey and nothing else all year, just like Pooh does in the books | Collect honey in pots for winter | Keep bees as pets for honey
test: Count them: a mother bear and 2 cubs walk past. How many claws do the three of them have in all? | 60 | 20 | 40 | 30
test: «bears hibernate same as dormice yeah, totally out cold» What is true? | No, a bear's body cools only a few degrees and it can wake fast | Yes, bears go stone cold and freeze solid until the spring comes | Yes, nothing could wake a bear in winter | No, bears never sleep in winter
test: «just climb a tree if a bear comes lol easy» What is right? | Not safe; black bears climb very well and young brown bears can too | Yes, no bear can climb at all | Yes, bears cannot see up | Yes, but only on the highest branch
test: «ranger said 'a fed bear is a dead bear', thats harsh» Why do rangers say it? | Bears fed by people become dangerous and are often moved or killed | Food from people is poisonous to bears and they die soon after eating it | Fed bears get too fat to live | Rangers do not like bears
test: A camper leaves a cool box of sausages outside the tent. What could happen? | A bear could smell it from far off and learn that tents mean food | Nothing, bears cannot smell sausages | The bear would bring berries in return | The sausages would scare the bears away
test: What would happen if a bear's claws pulled in like a cat's while digging for roots? | They would be less useful for digging; long fixed claws work like a rake | It would dig much faster, because tucked-in claws stay sharp and never break | Nothing, bears never dig | It would have to dig with its teeth
test: In a cartoon a bear chases a hiker and the hiker escapes easily by running. What is the truth? | Bears can run about 50 kilometres an hour, faster than any person, so never run | Bears are slow and heavy and give up after a few steps, so running always works | Bears can only walk slowly | Bears are scared of people in boots
test: A bear stands up on two legs and sniffs. What is it most likely doing? | Getting a better look and smell of what is around | Getting ready to dance | Showing that it is about to attack for sure | Trying to stand like a person for fun
test: «a newborn panda is how small? mum's like 100 kilos» About how heavy is a cub a thousand times lighter than a 100-kilogram mother? | About 100 grams | About 10 kilograms | About 1 kilogram | About 1 gram only
test: Why are simple rules about wild animals, like «always climb a tree», often wrong? | Each kind of animal behaves differently, so advice depends on the kind and the place | Animals learn the rules from people and change what they do every single year | Rules are only for zoos | Simple rules are always right
test: «bears are basically giant meat-eating monsters right» What is true? | Most bears eat mostly plants; only the polar bear eats mostly meat | Yes, all bears eat only meat, and they hunt every single day | Yes, but only at night | No, bears eat only honey
exam: Identify the number of claws possessed by an adult bear across all four feet. | Twenty, these being non-retractable | Sixteen, these being retractable | Ten, these being confined to the forefeet | Eighteen, the hallux being clawless
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the majority of bear species. | It consists predominantly of plant matter, the polar bear being the principal exception | It consists exclusively of honey and the larvae of bees | It consists predominantly of the flesh of large mammals | It consists exclusively of fish taken during migration
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the winter dormancy of the black bear? | Body temperature falls by only a few degrees and the animal may rouse rapidly, without eating or excreting for months | Body temperature falls to that of the surrounding snow, the heart ceases to beat and arousal before spring is impossible | The animal remains active and feeds throughout the winter | The animal must emerge each day in order to drink
exam: Ascertain the conduct appropriate to a person who observes a bear in a national park. | To remain calm, keep a considerable distance, refrain from running and follow the advice of the rangers | To flee at the greatest possible speed and ascend the nearest tree, which no bear is able to climb | To approach the animal in order to photograph it | To offer the animal food so as to pacify it
